From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: back-port "Preserve held-key releases after Esc" (b642cf8610, M160)

Back-port of upstream chromium commit
b642cf86103506df80aa4768322e02b6e001c5a5 (crbug.com/577459425,
crrev.com/c/8668604, refs/heads/main@{#1746972}). Production code only;
the upstream unit test changes are not included.

Stock 156 behaviour
(content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc,
RenderWidgetHostImpl::ForwardKeyboardEventWithCommands): when a
RawKeyDown is handled by the browser (KeyPressListenersHandleEvent() or
delegate_->PreHandleKeyboardEvent() returning HANDLED),
suppress_events_until_keydown_ is left set, and from then on EVERY KeyUp
and Char event is dropped before it reaches the renderer until the next
RawKeyDown/KeyDown arrives, whichever key it belongs to:

    if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyUp ||
        key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kChar) {
      ...
      return;
    }

In Electron PreHandleKeyboardEvent() returns HANDLED for Esc while the
page holds pointer lock (ExclusiveAccessManager::HandleUserKeyEvent) and
for any key whose "before-input-event" listener called preventDefault().
So in the game: hold W, press Esc (or a client keybind that is
preventDefault()ed), release W -> the W keyup never reaches the page and
the movement key stays logically held until W is pressed again.

What the patch changes (identical to upstream): the browser-handled key
is remembered by physical key (ui::DomCode) in a new
base::flat_set<ui::DomCode> suppressed_keys_, and only the KeyUp of a key
in that set is swallowed. KeyUp events of other keys are forwarded
normally. Char suppression is unchanged (still driven by
suppress_events_until_keydown_), and the Esc KeyUp is still passed to
PreHandleKeyboardEvent() for the press-and-hold Esc handling. The set is
cleared in SetupInputRouter() together with the flag.

Gating: none. This is a straight back-port and upstream landed it
ungated, so it is kept ungated here (no base::Feature). The added and
removed lines are the same as the upstream render_widget_host_impl.cc/.h
hunks; only the surrounding context differs (156 has no weak_this /
DismissUnboundedSurfaceOnKeyDown in the PreHandleKeyboardEvent switch).
The CHECK that a non-KeyUp/Char event seen while suppressing is a
RawKeyDown/KeyDown is removed, as upstream did.

Behaviour notes for the build agent:
- A KeyUp for a key whose keydown was preventDefault()ed is still not
  forwarded and still does not produce a "before-input-event" keyUp
  (same as stock).
- "before-input-event" keyUp events for OTHER keys released after a
  handled keydown are now emitted (stock swallowed them before
  PreHandleKeyboardEvent()).

A/B: no switch (ungated). Test with the client keybinds: hold W, tap a
preventDefault()ed key or Esc while pointer-locked, release W; in the
page log keydown/keyup. Stock: no keyup for W. Patched: keyup for W
arrives.

Platforms: all (browser process, UI thread, platform-independent code).
Not compiled; ui::DomCode and base::flat_set are already used by this
header (keyboard_keys_to_lock_).

diff --git a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
index 7b5c2d36a..e5c8ebbe3 100644
--- a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
+++ b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
@@ -1883,32 +1883,38 @@ void RenderWidgetHostImpl::ForwardKeyboardEventWithCommands(
     }
   }
 
+  const ui::DomCode dom_code = static_cast<ui::DomCode>(key_event.dom_code);
+
   // First, let keypress listeners take a shot at handling the event.  If a
   // listener handles the event, it should not be propagated to the renderer.
   if (KeyPressListenersHandleEvent(key_event)) {
     // Some keypresses that are accepted by the listener may be followed by Char
     // and KeyUp events, which should be ignored.
     suppress_events_until_keydown_ = true;
+    suppressed_keys_.insert(dom_code);
     return;
   }
 
-  if (suppress_events_until_keydown_) {
-    // If the preceding RawKeyDown event was handled by the browser, then we
-    // need to suppress all events generated by it until the next RawKeyDown or
-    // KeyDown event.
-    // However, always prehandle the Esc KeyUp events because they are needed to
+  // Suppress Char events after a browser-handled keydown until the next
+  // RawKeyDown or KeyDown event.
+  if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kChar &&
+      suppress_events_until_keydown_) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyUp &&
+      suppressed_keys_.erase(dom_code)) {
+    // Always prehandle the Esc KeyUp events because they are needed to
     // manage press-and-hold Esc key shortcuts for exiting browser fullscreen
     // and keyboard lock.
-    if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyUp ||
-        key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kChar) {
-      if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyUp &&
-          key_event.windows_key_code == ui::VKEY_ESCAPE) {
-        delegate_->PreHandleKeyboardEvent(key_event);
-      }
-      return;
+    if (key_event.windows_key_code == ui::VKEY_ESCAPE) {
+      delegate_->PreHandleKeyboardEvent(key_event);
     }
-    CHECK(key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kRawKeyDown ||
-          key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyDown);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kRawKeyDown ||
+      key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kKeyDown) {
     suppress_events_until_keydown_ = false;
   }
 
@@ -1916,12 +1922,12 @@ void RenderWidgetHostImpl::ForwardKeyboardEventWithCommands(
 
   // Only pre-handle the key event if it's not handled by the input method.
   if (delegate_ && !key_event.skip_if_unhandled) {
-    // We need to set |suppress_events_until_keydown_| to true if
-    // PreHandleKeyboardEvent() handles the event, but |this| may already be
-    // destroyed at that time. So set |suppress_events_until_keydown_| true
-    // here, then revert it afterwards when necessary.
+    // PreHandleKeyboardEvent() may destroy `this`, so set the suppression flag
+    // and insert the key into the set before calling it. Undo both afterwards
+    // if the keydown is not handled.
     if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kRawKeyDown) {
       suppress_events_until_keydown_ = true;
+      suppressed_keys_.insert(dom_code);
     }
 
     // Tab switching/closing accelerators aren't sent to the renderer to avoid
@@ -1945,6 +1951,7 @@ void RenderWidgetHostImpl::ForwardKeyboardEventWithCommands(
 
     if (key_event.GetType() == WebKeyboardEvent::Type::kRawKeyDown) {
       suppress_events_until_keydown_ = false;
+      suppressed_keys_.erase(dom_code);
     }
   }
 
@@ -4335,6 +4342,7 @@ void RenderWidgetHostImpl::SetupRenderInputRouter() {
 
 void RenderWidgetHostImpl::SetupInputRouter() {
   suppress_events_until_keydown_ = false;
+  suppressed_keys_.clear();
   monitoring_composition_info_ = false;
   GetRenderInputRouter()->SetupInputRouter(GetScaleFactorForView(view_.get()));
 }
diff --git a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.h b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.h
index a9ad4bee3..555804706 100644
--- a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.h
+++ b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.h
@@ -1569,19 +1569,25 @@ class CONTENT_EXPORT RenderWidgetHostImpl
   bool text_direction_updated_ = false;
   base::i18n::TextDirection text_direction_ = base::i18n::LEFT_TO_RIGHT;
 
-  // Indicates if Char and KeyUp events should be suppressed or not. Usually all
-  // events are sent to the renderer directly in sequence. However, if a
-  // RawKeyDown event was handled by PreHandleKeyboardEvent() or
+  // Indicates if Char events should be suppressed or not. Usually all events
+  // are sent to the renderer directly in sequence. However, if a RawKeyDown
+  // event was handled by PreHandleKeyboardEvent() or
   // KeyPressListenersHandleEvent(), e.g. as an accelerator key, then the
   // RawKeyDown event is not sent to the renderer, and the following sequence of
-  // Char and KeyUp events should also not be sent. Otherwise the renderer will
-  // see only the Char and KeyUp events and cause unexpected behavior. For
-  // example, pressing alt-2 may let the browser switch to the second tab, but
-  // the Char event generated by alt-2 may also activate a HTML element if its
-  // accesskey happens to be "2", then the user may get confused when switching
-  // back to the original tab, because the content may already have changed.
+  // Char events should also not be sent. Otherwise the renderer will see only
+  // the Char events and cause unexpected behavior. For example, pressing alt-2
+  // may let the browser switch to the second tab, but the Char event generated
+  // by alt-2 may also activate a HTML element if its accesskey happens to be
+  // "2", then the user may get confused when switching back to the original
+  // tab, because the content may already have changed.
   bool suppress_events_until_keydown_ = false;
 
+  // This set holds the physical keys whose KeyDown was handled by
+  // PreHandleKeyboardEvent() or KeyPressListenersHandleEvent(). Suppress their
+  // KeyUp events until released, without suppressing releases of other held
+  // keys.
+  base::flat_set<ui::DomCode> suppressed_keys_;
+
   bool pointer_lock_raw_movement_ = false;
   // Stores the keyboard keys to lock while waiting for a pending lock request.
   std::optional<base::flat_set<ui::DomCode>> keyboard_keys_to_lock_;
